Spring Boot + Microservices Interview Q&A
Target: 5 years experience, Java Full Stack Developer | Based on Spring Boot 3.2.x
Answer format to practise: Definition → Why → Internals → Code/Example → Production angle → Trade-
offs.
Module 1: Spring & Boot Fundamentals
1. Spring Framework vs Spring Boot? Spring is the core IoC/DI container plus modules (MVC, Data, Security,
AOP). Boot is an opinionated layer on top: starters, auto-configuration, embedded server, externalized config
and Actuator, so you get a production-ready app with minimal setup.
2. What is a starter? A curated dependency descriptor (e.g. spring-boot-starter-web brings Spring MVC,
Jackson, validation and embedded Tomcat). It removes version and compatibility juggling because the Boot
BOM manages versions.
3. How does the embedded server work? The server (Tomcat by default for MVC) runs inside the app's JVM
as a library, started by SpringApplication.run() . You ship one executable (fat) JAR with java -jar .
Swap to Jetty/Undertow by excluding the Tomcat starter and adding the other.
4. What does @SpringBootApplication contain? @SpringBootConfiguration (a @Configuration ),
@EnableAutoConfiguration , and @ComponentScan (from the main class's package downward). Hence
the main class belongs in the root package; otherwise your beans/entities aren't found.
5. What happens in SpringApplication.run() ? Determines web app type (servlet/reactive/none), loads
listeners and initializers, prepares the Environment (properties and profiles), creates the
ApplicationContext , runs component scan and auto-configuration, registers beans, starts the embedded
server, then runs CommandLineRunner / ApplicationRunner beans.
6. MVC vs WebFlux? MVC is thread-per-request and blocking, simple and fine for most CRUD with JDBC/JPA.
WebFlux is non-blocking (Reactor Mono / Flux , Netty), best for high-concurrency I/O-bound work and
streaming. If both are on the classpath, MVC wins.
Module 2: Auto-configuration
7. How does auto-configuration work internally? Boot reads META-
INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports from jars on
the classpath. Each listed class is a @Configuration guarded by conditions ( @ConditionalOnClass ,
@ConditionalOnMissingBean , @ConditionalOnProperty ). Only those whose conditions pass get
registered.
8. What is "backing off"? Auto-configured beans typically use @ConditionalOnMissingBean . If you define
your own DataSource or ObjectMapper , Boot's default steps aside.
9. How do you debug it? Run with --debug or debug=true to print the conditions report (positive/negative
matches), or hit Actuator /conditions .